we will never see the likes of Nelson  Mandela again but let me say to the  young people of Africa and the young  people around the world you too can make  his life work your own Madiba was  emerged as the last great liberator of  the 20th century like Gandhi he would  lead in the resistance movement like dr.  King he would give potent voice to the  claims of the impressed and the moral  necessity of racial justice emerging  from prison without the force of arms he  would like Abraham Lincoln hold his  country together when it threatened to  break apart and like America's Founding  Fathers he would erect a constitutional  order to preserve freedom from neutral  generations over 30 years ago while  still a student I learned of Nelson  Mandela and the struggles taking place  in this beautiful land and it stirred  something in me  it woke me up to my responsibilities to  others and to myself and it sent me on  an improbable journey that finds me here  today as he showed in painstaking  negotiations to transfer power and draft  new laws he was not afraid to compromise  for the sake of a larger goal and  because he was not only a leader of a  movement but a skillful politician the  Constitution that emerged was worthy of  this multiracial democracy true to his  vision of laws that protect minority as  well as majority rights and the precious  freedoms of every South Africa there are  too many people who happily embrace  Madiba's legacy of racial reconciliation  but passionately resist even modest  reforms that would challenge chronic  poverty and growing inequality there are  too many leaders who claim solidarity  with Madiba struggle for freedom but do  not tolerate dissent from their own  people  it's tempting I think to remember Nelson  Mandela at my [ __ ] smiling and serene  detached from the tawdry affairs of  lesser men but Madiba himself strongly  resisted such a lifeless portrait
 

